  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 PM MDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nSatellite imagery shows a shortwave feature pushing through \nOregon today, bringing a combination of wind and precipitation \nto East Idaho late this afternoon into early Tuesday. Gusty \nwinds will increase this afternoon and evening through the \novernight hours as an associated cold front pushes through the \nregion. Widespread gusts of 30 to 40 mph are expected, though \nprobabilistic high-end guidance indicates a few locations could \nbriefly gust over 50 mph, mainly across portions of the Southern\nSawtooth and Raft River regions. Travel impacts due to gusty \ncrosswinds should remain localized to Interstate 84 from the \nI-84/I-86 split south to the Utah state line. We will continue \nto hold off on a Wind Advisory given the localized footprint of \nthe strongest gusts and less-favorable overnight timing.\n\nLight precipitation already evident on radar across western \nIdaho is poised to overspread the Central Mountains through the \nremainder of the afternoon, quickly shifting east this evening \nand overnight as a mix of valley rain and mountain snow. An \nisolated thunderstorm remains possible late this afternoon and \nearly evening. Precipitation totals remain in line with previous\nguidance, generally under 0.10 inches in the valleys and up to \n0.25 inches across higher terrain, especially along the \nContinental Divide and eastern highlands. Snow levels drop to \naround 6,000 feet overnight, allowing higher basins to pick up a\nlight dusting. Confidence remains low for any valley snow \naccumulations below 6,000 feet, though a few flurries may mix in\nearly Tuesday morning.\n\nThe upper trough ejects northeast early Tuesday, but East Idaho\nremains under broad troughing over the Pacific Northwest and \nNorthern Rockies. While a few isolated higher-elevation showers \nmay linger on Tuesday, a drying and slight warming trend will \ntake hold through Thursday. For the late-week system, ensemble \nmeans depict a closed low off the Pacific Northwest coast Friday\ntransitioning into an open trough over the Intermountain West \nby Sunday. Despite persistent cluster spread regarding the exact\ndepth and track, East Idaho looks to remain on the forward \nflank of the trough, supporting continued rounds of valley rain \nand mountain snow showers through the weekend with generally \nlight liquid amounts.",
